-08-13T11:43:25Z","closed_at":"2021-08-13T11:43:25Z","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Steps to reproduce\r\n\r\nSet up a one-to-many relationship, with `strict_loading` enabled:\r\n\r\n```rb\r\nclass User < ApplicationRecord\r\n  self.strict_loading_by_default = true\r\n\r\n  has_many :checks\r\nend\r\n\r\nclass Check < ApplicationRecord\r\n  self.strict_loading_by_default = true\r\n\r\n  belongs_to :user\r\nend\r\n```\r\n\r\nAnd try to access the `checks` on a new `User`:\r\n\r\n```rb\r\nuser = User.create!\r\nputs user.checks\r\n```\r\n\r\n### Expected behavior\r\n\r\nThis should not cause an error, or there should be a way to trigger the checks to be loaded. It looks like right now the only options are to directly set the relationship when the parent record is created:\r\n\r\n```rb\r\nuser = User.create!(checks: [])\r\n```\r\n\r\nOr to disable `strict_loading` entirely:\r\n\r\n```rb\r\nuser = User.create!\r\nuser.strict_loading!(false)\r\n```\r\n\r\nI'd rather not disable it, so for now I'll set the relationship to an empty array.\r\n\r\n### Actual behavior\r\n\r\nA strict loading error is triggered:\r\n\r\n```\r\nActiveRecord::StrictLoadingViolationError:\r\n  `User` is marked for strict_loading. The `Check` association named `:checks` cannot be lazily loaded.\r\n```\r\n\r\n### System configuration\r\n**Rails version**: 6.1.3\r\n\r\n**Ruby version**: 2.7.2","closed_by":{"login":"rails-bot[bot]","id":28418009,"node_id":"MDM6Qm90Mjg0MTgwMDk=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/2272?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/rails-bot%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/rails-bot","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rails-bot%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/rail